From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patryk Kowalczyk <papyszoo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 01:28:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: upstream's flat view, cutscene bars and comfort fades

Upstream's renderer.cpp has three presentations that the Vulkan backend
(renderer_vulkan.cpp) never grew, because it re-implements an older renderer:
the flat camera in the scope and Camera rune, the black-bar cutscene view, and
the game/snap-turn/roomscale comfort fades. The hook side is upstream's and
was already in this tree; only the presentation was missing.

Flat view: the Linux mono helper now includes IsFlatCameraModeActive() (and a
fade guard), as the Windows helper does, so the flat camera keeps the game's
projection instead of a per-eye stereo one. renderer_vulkan.cpp bakes the
flat image into both eye images as upstream's 2D quad shows it: the capture at
its own aspect, centred in the HudDistance/HudSize window, black around it.
UseBlackBarsForCutscenes=1 (default off) uses the same window for a
third-person cutscene. Both eyes get the same image, so there is no stereo.

Comfort fades: EndFrame now reads IsAnyFadeScreenVisible(),
GetRoomscaleFadeAmount() and the snap-turn times exactly as upstream does.
The submit thread applies the game fade (the 2D capture's corner texel) and the
snap/roomscale fade (black) over the eye image with one full-screen pass:
max(game, custom) and a lerp, exactly upstream's presentDepthHLSL, so the HUD
quad stays the normal 2D layer on top. The pass is created on first use and is
skipped if the device refuses it.

Review fixes: the fade state is published in the same latestMutex block as the
2D content it samples, so a fade cannot lead its texture. The flat/black-bar
state is latched once per game frame at the left-eye copy and both eyes use
that value. A flat frame is never reused for a HUD-only publish (upstream
renderer.cpp:54-66), so the window cannot outlive the scope, and the flat
frame submits no scene depth. The normal HUD quad keeps its 2.0 m/1.0 m
geometry; only the flat window uses HudDistance/HudSize.

One log line per feature on each switch: "Flat view on/off", "Cutscene black
bars on/off", "Comfort fade on/off".
